"p2-deopt-roundtrip.mst — WINARM sprint P2's end-to-end integration check
 (MIGRATION.md §3.2, `just gate-p02`).

 An smi overflow inside a WARMED method is the shortest path to a real
 uncommon trap: `n + n` compiles to a `adds`/`b.vs` pair whose overflow edge
 is an inline `brk #0xDE00` (`emit::emit_uncommon_trap`), so crossing
 SmallInteger maxVal in compiled code is a genuine deopt — materialize the
 interpreter frame, re-execute the add, promote to a LargePositiveInteger,
 and return the result to the compiled method's caller as if nothing had
 happened.

 The contract this file encodes is a DIFFERENCE, so run it both ways:

   MACVM_JIT=off          cargo run -- run scripts/p2-deopt-roundtrip.mst --world world
   MACVM_JIT=threshold=20 cargo run -- run scripts/p2-deopt-roundtrip.mst --world world

 Both must print the same four lines and exit 0. Before P2 landed the VEH,
 the second command died at once on Windows ARM64 with 0xC000001D
 (STATUS_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION — which is what a `brk` outside Microsoft's own
 0xF000 namespace raises here, NOT STATUS_BREAKPOINT; that measurement is
 the whole reason the handler is keyed the way it is).

 threshold=20, not the =2 this file originally used: rule 1 (the author's —
 the threshold is NEVER below `JIT_THRESHOLD_FLOOR` = 20, and `parse_jit`
 now clamps every sub-floor value loudly). The silent warm-up loop below
 pushes `f:` past the floor so the final call runs COMPILED and the trap is
 real rather than interpreted; the printed output stays the same four lines
 the JIT-off oracle produces."

Object subclass: DeoptRepro [
    DeoptRepro class >> f: n [ ^n + n ]
]

Transcript show: (DeoptRepro f: 1) printString. Transcript cr.
Transcript show: (DeoptRepro f: 2) printString. Transcript cr.
Transcript show: (DeoptRepro f: 3) printString. Transcript cr.
1 to: 25 do: [:i | DeoptRepro f: i ].
Transcript show: (DeoptRepro f: SmallInteger maxVal) printString. Transcript cr.
